ing, then A might be correct. Because work performance is the main focus, but other aspects are also considered. So A says \"the most important thing is actual performance, but other aspects... cannot be ignored.\" That seems to align with the law's statement. So A could be correct.\n\nOption B: \"As long as the work performance is outstanding, other things are not important.\" That seems too absolute. The law doesn't say that work performance is the only criterion. It's part of a comprehensive assessment. So even if work performance is good, other factors like virtue, ability, etc., might still play a role. So B is making a strong claim that other things aren't important, which might not be true. So B is probably incorrect.\n\nOption C: \"As a civil servant, virtue is the most important.\" The law emphasizes work performance but also mentions other aspects. The key here is that work performance is emphasized, not necessarily that it's the most important. For example, if someone has high work performance but low integrity, maybe their overall assessment would be lower. But does that mean virtue isn't the most important? Or that work performance is the main factor? The law says comprehensive, so all factors are considered. But the emphasis is on work performance. So does that mean work performance is the key component, but not the only one? If that's the case, then C is incorrect because it's claiming virtue is the most important, which might not be true. The law doesn't say that. So C is probably wrong.\n\nOption D: \"The virtue, ability, diligence, performance, and honest of civil servants are equally important.\" The law says emphasis on work performance, so it's not equally important. The other factors (veter, ability, etc.) are part of the comprehensive assessment but not emphasized to the same degree as work performance. So D is incorrect because it's saying all five are equally important, which contradicts the law's statement.\n\nSo, going back, A seems correc