      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's try to figure out this logical reasoning problem step by step. So, we have three pieces of information about the phone usage among the administrative department members. The question is asking which of the options A to D can be inferred from these facts. Let me break down each given point and see how they relate to each other.\n\nFirst, let's list out the given information:\n\n1. Xiao Wang dialed the phone of everyone in the administrative department.\n2. Xiao Li once called Xiao Zhao, but Xiao Zhao never called anyone else.\n3. People who have never answered calls from others in the administration department have never called others.\n\nNow, the options are:\n\nA) Xiao Zhao never answered a phone call from Xiao Li\nB) Xiao Li once answered a phone call from Xiao Wang\nC) Someone in the administration department once called Xiao Wang\nD) Xiao Wang answered the phone from everyone in the administration department\n\nHmm, let's start by analyzing each piece of information and see what conclusions we can draw.\n\nStarting with the first point: Xiao Wang dialed everyone in the department. That means Xiao Wang has made calls to all the members. So, if we consider Xiao Wang, he has outgoing calls to everyone. But does that mean he received calls? The problem doesn't specify whether Xiao Wang received any calls or not. So maybe we can't assume that.\n\nSecond, Xiao Li called Xiao Zhao, but Xiao Zhao didn't call anyone else. So Xiao Zhao received a call from Xiao Li, but Xiao Zhao didn't make any calls. So Xiao Zhao is a recipient but not a caller. That's important. So Xiao Zhao only received a call from Xiao Li, and no one else called him. So Xiao Zhao is not a caller, only a recipient.\n\nThird, the third statement: People who have never answered calls from others in the administration department have never called others. So, if someone hasn't answered any calls made by others, then they themselves haven't made any calls. Wait, that seems like a contrapositive. Let me rephrase that. If a person hasn't answered any calls from others, then they themselves haven't made any calls. Because if they had made a call, they would have received a call, right? Wait, maybe not necessarily. Wait, the statement is: \"People who have never answered calls from others in the administration department have never called others.\" So, if someone hasn't answered any calls from others, then they haven't called others. So, if they haven't answered any calls, they haven't made any. Because if they had made a call, they would have received a call. Wait, but maybe they could have made a call without receiving a call? Hmm, but in reality, when you make a call, someone has to answer it. Unless it's a recorded message or something, but the problem doesn't specify. So maybe we can assume that if you make a call, someone has to answer it. So if someone hasn't answered any calls, they can't have made any calls. Therefore, the third statement is equivalent to saying that anyone who hasn't answered any incoming calls hasn't made any outgoing calls. So, it's a way of saying that there's a mutual relationship between incoming and outgoing calls. If you didn't answer any, you didn't call anyone. So, the third statement is a bit of a tautology? Wait, maybe not. Let's think again.\n\nSuppose there's a person who never answered any calls from others. Then according to the third statement, they never called anyone. So, if someone didn't answer any calls, they didn't make any. So, the contrapositive would be: if someone made any calls, they must have answered at least one call. Wait, no. The original statement is: \"People who have never answered calls from others... have never called others.\" So, the negation would be: if someone has answered calls from others, then they have called others. Wait, maybe not. Let me use logical implications.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet A(x) be \"Person x has answered calls from others.\"\n\nLet C(x) be \"Person x has called others.\"\n\nThe third statement is: If A(x) is false, then C(x) is false.\n\nIn other words, \u00acA(x) \u2192 \u00acC(x). Which is equivalent to C(x) \u2192 A(x). So, if someone has called others, then they have also answered calls from others. So, the third statement is saying that making calls implies answering calls. So, if you've made a call, you must have received at least one call. But if you haven't made any calls, you might have received some, but the statement doesn't say anything about that. Wait, no. The third statement is only about people who haven't answered any calls. If a person hasn't answered any calls, then they haven't called anyone. So, the contrapositive is: if a person has called someone, then they must have received at least one call. So, the third statement is ensuring that anyone who has made a call has at least one incoming call. But if someone hasn't made any calls, the statement doesn't say anything about them. So, the third statement is a bit different. It's not a universal statement that everyone who hasn't answered any calls hasn't called anyone, but rather that people who haven't answered any calls haven't called anyone. So, if someone hasn't answered any calls, they can't have called anyone. But if someone has answered some calls, they might have called others as well.\n\nOkay, now let's start inferring.\n\nFirst, Xiao Wang has called everyone. So, Xiao Wang is a caller to everyone. But we don't know if he received any calls. So, Xiao Wang's status as a caller is certain, but his status as a responder (if he received any calls) is uncertain.\n\nXiao Li called Xiao Zhao, but Xiao Zhao didn't call anyone else. So, Xiao Zhao received a call from Xiao Li, but didn't make any calls. So, Xiao Zhao is a recipient but not a caller. So, Xiao Zhao is only in the recipient part of the communication.\n\nThird statement: As per the third statement, anyone who hasn't answered any calls from others hasn't called anyone. So, if someone hasn't answered any calls, they haven't called anyone. So, if someone has answered some calls, they might have called others as well. But if they haven't answered any, they haven't called any.\n\nNow, let's look at the options.\n\nOption A: Xiao Zhao never answered a phone call from Xiao Li.\n\nBut wait, Xiao Li called Xiao Zhao. So, Xiao Zhao did receive a call from Xiao Li. Therefore, Xiao Zhao has answered at least one call (from Xiao Li). Therefore, according to the third statement, since he has answered a call, he must have called someone. But the third statement says that people who have never answered calls from others have never called others. Wait, but Xiao Zhao has answered a call (from Xiao Li), so according to the contrapositive of the third statement, if he has answered a call, he must have called someone. So, Xiao Zhao must have called someone. But the third statement is about people who have never answered calls. So, if someone hasn't answered any calls, they haven't called anyone. But if someone has answered some calls, they might have called others. So, Xiao Zhao has answered a call (from Xiao Li), so he must have called someone. But according to the third statement, he hasn't called anyone. Wait, that's a contradiction. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's re-examine the third statement. The third statement is: \"People who have never answered calls from others in the administration department have never called others.\" So, if a person hasn't answered any calls from others, then they haven't called others. So, the contrapositive is: if a person has called others, then they must have answered at least one call. So, if someone has made a call, they must have received at least one call. But if someone hasn't made any calls, the statement doesn't say anything. So, in the case of Xiao Zhao, he has received a call (from Xiao Li), so according to the contrapositive, he must have also made at least one call. But according to the third s