on (seat 2), which is Li. But condition 2 says \"Only one person between Li and Zhao can attend the meeting.\" So Li and Zhao must have exactly one person between them. If Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 4, then there's seat 3 in between. That would be two people between them. So that would violate condition 2. Therefore, if Zhang is at seat 1, Zhao must be at seat 3, but then Li can't be at seat 2. Wait, but Li is attending. So maybe Li is somewhere else.\n\nWait, maybe I need to approach this differently. Let's try to map possible seats.\n\nLet me consider the seating arrangement. Since the problem doesn't specify the number of seats, but there are six people. However, the problem mentions \"the three among Zhang, Sun, and Zhou,\" so maybe they are in a separate group? Wait, no, the problem says \"two of the three among Zhang, Sun, and Zhou can attend.\" So out of the three, two will go. So maybe they are in the same group? Not sure. Maybe the seating is a line of six seats. Let's assume they are sitting in a line of six seats. Then, the positions are 1 to 6.\n\nLet me try to assign seats.\n\nFirst, let's suppose Zhang is at seat 1. Then, according to condition 1, Zhao must be two seats away. So Zhao could be at seat 3. Then, between Zhang (1) and Zhao (3), there's seat 2. So that's one person. So that satisfies condition 1. Then, Li must be at seat 2, because if Li is at seat 2, then between Li (2) and Zhao (3) is one person (seat 2 is Li, seat 3 is Zhao). Wait, no. If Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 3, then between them is seat 2 (Li) and seat 3 (Zhao). Wait, that's adjacent. Wait, no. If Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 4, then between them is seat 3. So that's one person. So maybe Zhao is at seat 4. Then, between Li (2) and Zhao (4) is seat 3. So that's one person. So that would satisfy condition 2. So Li at 2, Zhao at 4.\n\nNow, Wang is attending (since we're considering option A: Zhang and Wang). So Wang must be somewhere. Also, from condition 3, Zhang and Wang can't be adjacent. Zhang is at 1, so Wang can't be at 2. So Wang can be at 3,4,5,6. But Zhao is at 4. So Wang can't be at 4. So Wang can be at 3,5,6.\n\nBut from condition 4, since Wang is attending, Li must attend. Li is at 2. So that's okay.\n\nFrom condition 5, since Wang is attending, Li must attend. Which is already satisfied.\n\nFrom condition 6: If Zhao isn't attending, then Sun isn't attending. But Zhao is attending (condition 1), so condition 6 is satisfied.\n\nNow, we have Zhang (1), Li (2), Zhao (4), Wang (somewhere). Let's say Wang is at 3. Then, seat 3 is taken by Wang. Then, remaining people are Sun and Zhou. We need two of the three among Zhang, Sun, Zhou to attend. Wait, Zhang is already attending, so we need one more from Sun and Zhou. But the condition says \"two of the three among Zhang, Sun, and Zhou can attend.\" So Zhang is already one, so we need one more from Sun and Zhou. So either Sun or Zhou must attend. But we have two people left: Sun and Zhou. So one of them must attend. But we can only have two total. Wait, no. Wait, the problem says \"two of the three among Zhang, Sun, and Zhou can attend the meeting.\" So among the three (Zhang, Sun, Zhou), exactly two will attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou must attend. So either Sun or Zhou. But we have two people left (Sun and Zhou). So one of them must attend. But we can only have two total. Wait, no. Wait, the three people are Zhang, Sun, Zhou. Two of them will attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. So either Sun or Zhou. So one of them must attend. But we have two people left. Wait, no. Wait, the three people are Zhang, Sun, Zhou. So two of them will attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. So either Sun or Zhou. So one of them must attend. But we have two people left (Sun and Zhou). Wait, that can't be. Wait, maybe I misread. Let me check again.\n\nThe condition says: \"Two of the three among Zhang, Sun, and Zhou can attend the meeting.\" So among those three (Zhang, Sun, Zhou), exactly two will attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou must attend. So either Sun or Zhou. So one of them must attend. But we have two people left (Sun and Zhou). Wait, that's a problem. Because if we have to choose two from the three (Zhang, Sun, Zhou), and Zhang is already in, then one more from Sun and Zhou. So exactly two. So one of Sun or Zhou must attend. But there are two people left. So if we have to pick one, then the other won't attend. But the problem says \"two of the three can attend,\" meaning exactly two. So if Zhang is attending, then one more from Sun and Zhou. So either Sun or Zhou. So one of them must attend. But we have two people left. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, the three people are Zhang, Sun, Zhou. Two of them will attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. So exactly two. So one of them must attend. Therefore, the other one doesn't. So in this case, if we have Zhang (1), Li (2), Zhao (4), Wang (3), then the remaining people are Sun and Zhou. We need one more from them. So either Sun or Zhou must attend. But we can only have two total. Wait, no. Wait, the total number of people attending is Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, and one more from Sun and Zhou. So that's five people. But the problem says all six cannot attend. So five is okay. But the condition is that two of the three among Zhang, Sun, Zhou can attend. So among those three, two. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. So exactly two. So one of them must attend. Therefore, the other one doesn't. So in this case, if we have Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, and either Sun or Zhou, that's five people. But the problem allows up to five. So that's okay. So in this scenario, it's possible. So option A (Zhang and Wang) could be possible.\n\nBut let's check if there's a conflict. Let's see. If we have Zhang (1), Wang (3), Li (2), Zhao (4), and then Sun or Zhou. Let's say Sun is attending. Then the attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun. That's five people. Zhou doesn't attend. Is that okay? The problem says at least one person between Zhang and Wang. Zhang is at 1, Wang at 3. There's seat 2 between them. So that's okay. Between Li (2) and Zhao (4), there's seat 3 (Wang) and seat 4 (Zhao). Wait, no. Li is at 2, Zhao at 4. Between them is seat 3 (Wang). So there's one person (Wang) between Li and Zhao. But condition 2 says \"Only one person between Li and Zhao can attend the meeting.\" So Li and Zhao must have exactly one person between them. If Li is at 2 and Zhao at 4, then there's seat 3 (Wang) between them. That's one person. So that's okay. So condition 2 is satisfied. Also, between Zhang (1) and Zhao (4), there are seats 2,3,4. Wait, Zhang is at 1, Zhao at 4. Between them are seats 2,3,4. Wait, seat 4 is Zhao. So the distance is three seats apart. But condition 1 says \"Only one person between Zhang and Zhao can attend the meeting.\" So if Zhang is at 1 and Zhao at 4, there are two people between them (seats 2 and 3). That would violate condition 1. Oh no! Wait, that's a problem. So if Zhang is at 1 and Zhao at 4, there are two people between them (seats 2 and 3). But condition 1 requires exactly one person between them. So that's a conflict. Therefore, this arrangement is invalid.\n\nSo my initial assumption of placing Zhao at seat 4 with Zhang at 1 leads to a conflict. Therefore, maybe Zhao can't be at seat 4 if Zhang is at seat 1. Let's try another position for Zhao.\n\nIf Zhang is at seat 1, then Zhao must be two seats away. So possible positions for Zhao are seat 3 or seat 5. Let's try Zhao at seat 5. Then, between Zhang (1) and Zhao (5), there are seats 2,3,4. That's three people. But condition 1 says only one person between them. So that's not allowed. Therefore, Zhao can't be at seat 5. So Zhao must be at seat 3. Then, between Zhang (1) and Zhao (3), there's seat 2. So that's one person. So that's okay. Then, Li must be at seat 2. Because between Li and Zhao (seat 3), there's seat 2, which is Li. Wait, that's adjacent. Wait, no. If Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 3, then there's no one between them. That's adjacent. But condition 2 says \"Only one person between Li and Zhao can attend the meeting.\" So Li and Zhao must have exactly one person between them. So if Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 4, then there's seat 3 in between. That's one person. So that's okay. So maybe Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 4. Then, between Li (2) and Zhao (4) is seat 3. So that's one person. Okay.\n\nSo Zhang (1), Li (2), Zhao (4). Then, Wang is attending (since we're considering option A: Zhang and Wang). So Wang must be somewhere. From condition 3, Zhang and Wang can't be adjacent. Zhang is at 1, so Wang can't be at 2. So Wang can be at 3,4,5,6. But Zhao is at 4. So Wang can't be at 4. So Wang can be at 3,5,6.\n\nIf Wang is at 3, then seats 3 is taken. Then, remaining people are Sun and Zhou. We need two of the three among Zhang, Sun, Zhou to attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. So either Sun or Zhou. Let's say Sun is attending. Then the attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun. That's five people. Zhou doesn't attend. Check the conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (1) and Wang (3): seat 2 is Li. So one person. Okay.\n\n- Between Li (2) and Zhao (4): seat 3 is Wang. One person. Okay.\n\n- Between Zhang (1) and Zhao (4): seats 2,3,4. Wait, Zhang at 1, Zhao at 4. Between them are seats 2,3,4. That's three people. But condition 1 says only one person between them. So this is a conflict. Therefore, invalid.\n\nSo again, conflict. Therefore, if Zhao is at seat 4, then Zhang at 1 and Zhao at 4 would have three people between them. So that's invalid. Therefore, Zhao can't be at seat 4 if Zhang is at seat 1. Wait, but earlier we thought Zhao could be at seat 3. But when Zhao is at seat 3, then between Zhang (1) and Zhao (3) is seat 2. So that's one person. Then Li must be at seat 2. But then Li is adjacent to Zhao (seat 3). Wait, no. If Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 3, they are adjacent. But condition 2 says \"Only one person between Li and Zhao can attend the meeting.\" So Li and Zhao must have exactly one person between them. If Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 4, then there's seat 3 in between. That's one person. So that's okay. So maybe Li is at seat 2 and Zhao at seat 4. Then, between Li (2) and Zhao (4) is seat 3. So that's one person. Okay.\n\nSo Zhang (1), Li (2), Zhao (4). Then, Wang is attending (option A). So Wang can be at 3,5,6. Let's try Wang at 5. Then, seats 5 is taken. Then, remaining people are Sun and Zhou. We need two of the three (Zhang, Sun, Zhou) to attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. Let's say Sun is attending. Then attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun. That's five people. Zhou doesn't attend. Check conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (1) and Wang (5): seats 2,3,4. Three people. But condition 3 says at least one person between Zhang and Wang. So they can't be adjacent. But Zhang is at 1 and Wang at 5. They are not adjacent. So that's okay. Wait, the condition says \"at least one person between Zhang and Wang,\" which means they can't be adjacent. Since Zhang is at 1 and Wang at 5, there are seats 2,3,4 in between. So that's three people. So they are separated by three seats. So that's more than one person. So the condition is satisfied. Okay.\n\n- Between Li (2) and Zhao (4): seat 3 is Wang. One person. Okay.\n\n- Between Zhang (1) and Zhao (4): seats 2,3,4. Three people. But condition 1 says only one person between them. So this is a conflict. Therefore, invalid.\n\nAgain, conflict. So regardless of where we place Zhao, if Zhang is at seat 1, we end up with a conflict with condition 1. Therefore, maybe Zhang can't be at seat 1. Let's try placing Zhang at seat 2.\n\nIf Zhang is at seat 2, then according to condition 1, Zhao must be two seats away. So possible positions for Zhao are seat 4 or seat 0 (which doesn't exist). So Zhao must be at seat 4. Then, between Zhang (2) and Zhao (4) is seat 3. So one person. Okay.\n\nThen, Li must be at seat 3, because between Li and Zhao (seat 4), there's seat 3. So Li at 3. Then, Li is at 3. Now, Wang is attending (option A). So Wang must be somewhere. From condition 3, Zhang (2) and Wang can't be adjacent. So Wang can't be at 1 or 3. Since Zhang is at 2, Wang can't be at 1 or 3. But Li is at 3. So Wang can't be at 3. So Wang can be at 4,5,6. But Zhao is at 4. So Wang can be at 5 or 6.\n\nIf Wang is at 5, then seats 5 is taken. Then, remaining people are Sun and Zhou. We need two of the three (Zhang, Sun, Zhou) to attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. Let's say Sun is attending. Then attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun. That's five people. Zhou doesn't attend. Check conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (2) and Wang (5): seats 3,4. Two people. So that's okay, since the condition says at least one person between them. They are not adjacent.\n\n- Between Li (3) and Zhao (4): seat 3 is Li, seat 4 is Zhao. Adjacent. But condition 2 says \"Only one person between Li and Zhao.\" If Li and Zhao are adjacent, there's no one between them. So that violates condition 2. Therefore, invalid.\n\nSo Li and Zhao can't be adjacent. Therefore, if Li is at 3 and Zhao at 4, they are adjacent, which is not allowed. Therefore, this arrangement is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, if Wang is at 6. Then, seats 6 is taken. Remaining people: Sun and Zhou. Again, two of the three (Zhang, Sun, Zhou) must attend. So one of Sun or Zhou. Let's say Sun is attending. Then attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun. Check conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (2) and Wang (6): seats 3,4,5. Three people. So they are separated by three seats. The condition says \"at least one person between Zhang and Wang,\" which is satisfied. Okay.\n\n- Between Li (3) and Zhao (4): seat 3 is Li, seat 4 is Zhao. Adjacent. Again, violates condition 2. So invalid.\n\nSame problem. Therefore, placing Zhang at seat 2 leads to Li and Zhao being adjacent, which is not allowed. Therefore, Zhang can't be at seat 2. Let's try Zhang at seat 3.\n\nIf Zhang is at seat 3, then Zhao must be two seats away. So possible positions for Zhao are seat 1 or seat 5. Let's try Zhao at seat 1. Then, between Zhang (3) and Zhao (1): there are seats 2 and 4 between them. Wait, no. If Zhang is at 3 and Zhao at 1, the distance is two seats apart. So seats 2 and 4 are between them? Wait, no. If you're sitting in a line, the distance between seat 1 and seat 3 is two seats: 1, 2, 3. So the distance is two seats. So between them is seat 2. So if Zhang is at 3 and Zhao at 1, there's seat 2 between them. So that's one person. Okay.\n\nThen, Li must be at seat 2, because between Li and Zhao (seat 1), there's seat 2. So Li at 2. Now, Wang is attending (option A). So Wang must be somewhere. From condition 3, Zhang (3) and Wang can't be adjacent. So Wang can't be at 2 or 4. Zhang is at 3, so Wang can't be at 2 or 4. Li is at 2, so Wang can't be at 2. Therefore, Wang can be at 5 or 6.\n\nIf Wang is at 5, then seats 5 is taken. Remaining people: Sun and Zhou. We need two of the three (Zhang, Sun, Zhou) to attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. Let's say Sun is attending. Then attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun. Check conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (3) and Wang (5): seats 4. One person. Okay.\n\n- Between Li (2) and Zhao (1): seat 2 is Li, seat 1 is Zhao. Adjacent. But condition 2 says \"Only one person between Li and Zhao.\" If they are adjacent, there's no one between them. So this violates condition 2. Therefore, invalid.\n\nAlternatively, if Wang is at 6. Then, seats 6 is taken. Remaining people: Sun and Zhou. Again, one of them must attend. Attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, and either Sun or Zhou. Check conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (3) and Wang (6): seats 