ct. So Zhang can't be at seat 3. Let's try Zhang at seat 4.\n\nIf Zhang is at seat 4, then Zhao must be two seats away. So possible positions for Zhao are seat 2 or seat 6. Let's try Zhao at seat 2. Then, between Zhang (4) and Zhao (2): seats 3 and 5 in between. Wait, no. If Zhang is at 4 and Zhao at 2, the distance is two seats apart. So seats 3 and 5 are between them? No, in a line, seat 4 to seat 2 is two seats: 4,3,2. So the distance is two seats. So between them is seat 3. So one person. Okay.\n\nThen, Li must be at seat 3, because between Li and Zhao (2), there's seat 3. So Li at 3. Now, Wang is attending (option A). So Wang must be somewhere. From condition 3, Zhang (4) and Wang can't be adjacent. So Wang can't be at 3 or 5. Li is at 3, so Wang can't be at 3. Therefore, Wang can be at 1,2,5,6. But Zhao is at 2. So Wang can be at 1,5,6.\n\nIf Wang is at 1. Then, seats 1 is taken. Remaining people: Sun and Zhou. We need two of the three (Zhang, Sun, Zhou) to attend. Since Zhang is already attending, one more from Sun and Zhou. Let's say Sun is attending. Then attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun. Check conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (4) and Wang (1): seats 2,3. Two people. Okay.\n\n- Between Li (3) and Zhao (2): seat 3 is Li, seat 2 is Zhao. Adjacent. Violates condition 2. So invalid.\n\nIf Wang is at 5. Then, seats 5 is taken. Remaining people: Sun and Zhou. Again, one of them must attend. Attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, and either Sun or Zhou. Check conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (4) and Wang (5): seat 4 is Zhang, seat 5 is Wang. Adjacent. Violates condition 3. So invalid.\n\nIf Wang is at 6. Then, seats 6 is taken. Remaining people: Sun and Zhou. Again, one of them must attend. Attendees are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, and either Sun or Zhou. Check conditions:\n\n- Between Zhang (4) and Wang (6): seats 5. One person. Okay.\n\n- Between Li (3) and Zhao (2): seat 3 is Li, seat 2 is Zhao. Adjacent. Violates condition 2. So invalid.\n\nTherefore, placing Zha