n\nIn the example I constructed, the order is M2, M1, P, E. But looking at the options, option D is \"Major two, major one, English, politics.\" Wait, that's M2, M1, E, P. But in my example, it's M2, M1, P, E. So that's different. So perhaps my example isn't matching any of the options. Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the order is M1, M2, P, E. But according to the options, there's no such option. The options are:\n\nA) Major one, major two, English, politics.\n\nB) Major two, major one, politics, English.\n\nC) Major one, major two, politics, English.\n\nD) Major two, major one, English, politics.\n\nSo in my example, the order is M2, M1, P, E. But none of the options have M2 first. Wait, but the options are the possible correct rankings. So perhaps my approach is wrong.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in assuming M2 is higher than M1. Let's try another approach. Let's suppose that M1 is the highest, then P, then E, then M2. But according to the first equation, P + M1 = E + M2. If M1 is highest, P next, then E, then M2. Then M2 would be equal to P + M1 - E. If M1 is much higher than P and E, then M2 could be higher than M1. For example, if M1=10, P=6, E=4. Then M2=6+10-4=12. So M2=12. Then the order would be M1=10, M2=12, P=6, E=4. But then M2 is higher than M1, which contradicts the third condition that M1 > P + E. Wait, M1=10, P + E=6 +4=10. So M1=10 is not greater than P + E=10. It's equal. But the third condition says M1 > P + E. So this is invalid.\n\nSo in this case, M1=10 is not greater than P + E=10. So that's a problem. Therefore, M1 must be strictly greater than P + E. So if we take M1=11, P=6, E=4. Then M2=6 +11 -4=13. Then M1=11 > P + E=10. That works. Then the order is M2=13, M1=11, P=6, E=4. But again, M2 is higher than M1, which contradicts the third condition. Wait, no. Wait, M1=11 is greater than P + E=10. So the order is M2=13, M1=11, P=6, E=4. But in this case, M2 is higher than M1. So again, the order is M2, M1, P, E. But according to the options, there's no such option. The options are:\n\nA) Major one, major two, English, politics.\n\nB) Major two, major one, politics, English.\n\nC) Major one, major two, politics, English.\n\nD) Major two, major one, English, politics.\n\nSo none of these options have M2 first. But according to our analysis, M1 is the highest. Wait, but in the options, the first subject in each option is either major one, major two, or English. So maybe there's a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, let's check the problem statement again. The four subjects are politics, English, major one, major two. The options are:\n\nA) Major one, major two, English, politics.\n\nB) Major two, major one, politics, English.\n\nC) Major one, major two, politics, English.\n\nD) Major two, major one, English, politics.\n\nSo all options start with either major one, major two, or English. But according to our analysis, M1 is the highest, so the first subject should be M1. But none of the options start with M1. So this suggests that perhaps my earlier conclusion that M1 is the highest is incorrect. But how?\n\nWait, let's re-examine the third condition: M1 > P + E. This means that M1 is greater than the sum of P and E. Therefore, M1 must be greater than both P and E individually. Because if M1 were equal to P or E, then M1 couldn't be greater than their sum. For example, if M1 = P, then M1 > P + E would imply P > P + E, which is impossible. Similarly, if M1 = E, then E > P + E, which implies P > 0, but not necessarily. So M1 must be greater than both P and E individually. Therefore, M1 is the highest score. Therefore, the first subject in the ranking should be M1. But none of the options have M1 first. So this is a problem. Therefore, either the options are wrong, or my reasoning is wrong.\n\nWait, let's check the options again. The options are:\n\nA) Major one, major two, English, politics.\n\nB) Major two, major one, politics, English.\n\nC) Major one, major two, politics, English.\n\nD) Major two, majo