istration is not here. So, unless there's a connection between first-grade and third-grade students, we can't assume they are related. Therefore, B might not be correct either.\n\nOption C: Some students with household registration in this city are third-grade students. Well, the problem says all third-grade students are registered here. So, every third-grader has a household registration in S City. Therefore, there are definitely some third-grade students with household registration here. So, this seems like a valid conclusion. C looks correct.\n\nOption D: Some first-grade students are not children of migrant workers. The problem states that some first-grade students are children of migrant workers, but their household registration isn't here. Wait, does that mean all first-graders are not children of migrants? No, the problem says some first-graders are children of migrants, but their registration isn't here. So, there exists at least one first-grader who is a child of a migrant but not registered here. But does that mean that some first-graders are not children of migrants? Not necessarily. Because the problem only tells us that some are, but others might be. So, we can't conclude that all first-graders are not children of migrants. Therefore, D might not be correct.\n\nSo, going back through the options, the only one that can be definitively concluded is C. All third-grade students are registered here, so some of the students with household registration in the city must be third-graders. Therefore, the answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe problem states that all third-grade students are registered in S City, some second-grade students are as well, and some first-grade students are children of migrant workers (but not registered locally). Analyzing the options:\n\n- **A** is incorrect because the information only indicates some second-graders are registered, not all.  \n- **B** is incorrect because the non-registration of first-grade children\u2019s registration