      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about an insurance company that wants to issue a new policy to cover elderly health services but is worried about not having enough income to cover claims. The question is asking which strategy would minimize their losses, and the options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the company's concerns. They mentioned that premiums need to be low to attract customers, but if the claims are high, they might not make enough money. So the main issue here is risk selection\u2014how to choose which customers to insure so that the expected payouts are less than the premiums they charge.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Insuring only those who didn't have serious diseases as children. Hmm. If they're excluding people who had childhood diseases, maybe that reduces the risk of high claims? But wait, the problem states that the policy is for elderly people who need services. If someone had a childhood disease, maybe they have a higher chance of needing services later? Or maybe not. Wait, the reasoning here is that by excluding people with certain pre-existing conditions (even if those conditions are related to childhood), they can lower the risk pool. But the options mention \"did not suffer any serious diseases as children.\" So if they exclude people who had serious diseases as children, perhaps those people are more likely to have health issues later in life? Or maybe not. Wait, maybe the idea is that if you exclude people with certain health conditions, you reduce the number of high-risk individuals in the insured group, thereby lowering the expected payout costs. But the problem is that the policy is for elderly people who need services. So maybe the people they are insuring are elderly, but if they had childhood diseases, they might still be in the risk pool. Wait, but if they are excluding people who had serious diseases as children, maybe those people are less likely to need services in their elderly years? Or maybe the exclusion is to prevent people with certain health issues from being insured, thereby reducing the overall risk.\n\nAlternatively, option C says to insure only those rejected by other companies. If other companies are rejecting certain people, that might mean those people are high risk, so by insuring them, the new company can avoid underwriting and just take on the highest risk customers. But would that minimize losses? If they are insuring high risk customers, the expected payout would be higher, so their losses (payouts minus premiums) would be higher. So that might not be good. So maybe C is not the answer.\n\nOption D says attracting middle-aged customers unlikely to submit claims for many years. Middle-aged might be a risk group because they are entering the peak of their health, but if they are unlikely to make many claims, then the expected payout would be lower. So if they target middle-aged people who don't make many claims, the company can have lower costs. That seems like a good strategy to minimize losses. So D might be the answer.\n\nOption B is insuring only the wealthy. That would mean higher risk pool (richer people might have higher healthcare costs) but lower risk pool (they can't pay high claims). Wait, but if they are insuring only the wealthy, maybe the expected payout is lower because they can charge high premiums. But the problem is that low-income customers might be excluded, but the question is about minimizing losses. If they charge high premiums but only insure people who can afford it (the wealthy), then the expected payout might be lower. But is that the case? Wait, the problem is about the company's losses. If they charge high premiums but only insure people who can pay, then the actual claims would be from people who can pay, so the expected payout is high. But if they charge low premiums, they might have to insure everyone, including those who can't pay, leading to higher expected payout. So maybe the optimal is to balan