ociety more just.\" So the main idea is using rules to guide and regulate competition, which is A. The core idea might be C, but the question is asking for the main content of the text. The text is explaining the role of rules in achieving these other goals. So the main point is A.\n</think>\n\nThe context provided revolves around the role of strict rules in ancient Greek competitions, emphasizing how they prevent disputes and ensure fair outcomes. The question asks for the main content of this text. Among the options:\n\n- **A) The importance of rules in competitions** directly aligns with the text's focus on guiding, regulating, and eliminating conflicts through strict rules.  \n- **B) Fairness as a persistent topic** is secondary, as the text does not explicitly address fairness beyond the role of competition rules.  \n- **C) Abiding by rules as a core idea** centers the competition structure itself, but the text\u2019s core purpose is explaining why rules are necessary, not their procedural core.  \n- **D) Only strict rules for social justice** is unrelated to the text\u2019s focus on practical competition management rather than social equity.  \n\nThe most comprehensive answer is **A**, as the text explicitly emphasizes the critical role of rules in competitions without mentioning fairness, social justice, or procedural core.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n\\boxed{A}",
