ally in complex situations. It highlights existing **methods** and **principles** that have been tested over time, indicating a body of knowledge and guidance to apply reason effectively. \n\n- **Option A** claims reason is \"the most reliable,\" but the passage doesn't exclusively elevate reason over other tools.  \n- **Option C** criticizes irrational tools but focuses narrowly on tools rather than the established methods described.  \n- **Option D** asserts rational judgments are \"never wrong,\" which contradicts the passage's focus on reliability rather than infallibility.  \n\n**Option B** directly supports the passage's theme: \"a set of methods that can be used to determine what is appropriate and true.\" This aligns with the text's assertion of tested methods and principles.  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{B}",
