cause even though we don't know, in reality, they might be better. Therefore, C would weaken the original argument. Because the original argument was that we can't assume they are less harmful, but if in reality they have less of the harmful ingredient, then they are actually less harmful. Therefore, the original argument is not just about uncertainty but also possible reality. So C would make the original argument weaker because it shows that even without knowing, they might still be better. Therefore, C is a correct answer.\n\nD) Laundry detergents advertised as environmentally friendly are typically less effective than other detergents, so that larger amounts must be used.\n\nThis is saying that ecologically friendly detergents are less effective, hence requiring more detergent. But the original argument is about their environmental harm, not their effectiveness. So if D is true, it would talk about effectiveness, which is a different issue. The original argument is about environmental harm, so D's statement about effectiveness might not directly affect the argument. Therefore, D might not be the correct answer.\n\nSo, to recap:\n\n- A: Talks about other ingredients being worse than surfactants. If that's true, then even if ecologically friendly have different ingredients, they might still be worse. So the original argument is correct. Therefore, A does not weaken the argument.\n\n- B: Says non-ecologically friendly don't harm the environment significantly. If true, that doesn't affect the original argument because the original argument is about ecologically friendly vs. non-ecologically friendly. Even if non-ecologically friendly are not harmful, ecologically friendly could still be less harmful. So B doesn't weaken the argument.\n\n- C: Says ecologically friendly have much lower amounts of surfactants. If that's true, then even though we don't know about other ingredients, having less surfactant (which might be better) would support the idea that they are less 