ed appropriately, maybe that would help. But the statement is about diesel not increasing emissions. So if you improve diesel quality, emissions would decrease. So C could support the statement. But also, price monitoring: if diesel is cheaper, people might buy more, but the statement is about not increasing emissions. So if you set a lower price for diesel, but also improve quality, maybe that's better. But the option says \"price monitoring methods to encourage production\". Maybe if prices are kept high, production might not be encouraged. Hmm. Not sure. Maybe C is possible, but let's check D.\n\nOption D: Enhance independent innovation in advanced diesel tech, develop high-quality diesel vehicles. This supports the idea that by improving technology, diesel vehicles can have lower emissions. The statement says that diesel can make no increase in emissions or even reduce them. So if you innovate and produce better diesel vehicles, emissions would be better. So D seems to directly support the statement. The other options are either about using existing diesel (like B) or changing prices (A) which might not directly lead to emission reduction. C is about improving quality and pricing, but D is about innovation and development, which would lead to better emissions. So D is probably the correct answer.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The problem says \"which of the following options best supports the above statement\". The statement is that diesel can make no increase in vehicle pollutants emissions or even emission reductions. So the options need to be about measures that would lead to diesel vehicles having lower emissions than gasoline, possibly even reducing emissions. \n\nOption C mentions improving diesel quality and using price monitoring. If diesel is made cleaner and priced right, maybe that would lead to lower emissions. But the key is that the statement is about diesel not increasing emissions. So if you improve diesel's quality (i.e., make them cleaner), 