      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about a survey that found more households moving out of Weston than moving in, two to one. The conclusion is that the population of Weston will decline next year's census because people don't update their addresses. The question is which option, if true, would most help strengthen that argument.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the argument structure. The main point is that because not enough people are updating their addresses, the census will underestimate the population loss. So the argument is that lack of address changes leads to an undercount in the census. The options are about why people might not update their addresses.\n\nOption A says many people moving into Weston didn't notify either office. If that's true, that would mean more people are not updating their addresses, which would make the current count lower than the actual population. That would support the argument that the census undercounts, hence the population seems to decline. So A could be a good option.\n\nOption B talks about the composition of people moving in and out. If more young adults are moving out and fewer adults are moving in, maybe the population loss is due to a different age group. But how does that affect the address changes? If more adults are moving out, but they are the ones changing addresses, while the remaining adults (who are staying or moving in) are older, maybe the number of people changing addresses is actually higher? Wait, but the argument is that households moving out outnumber those moving in two to one. So if more households are moving out, but some of those households might be parents with children (as in option D), but the problem is about individual households. Wait, the survey is about households, so each household is a separate unit. So if more households are moving out than moving in, that's two to one. But if some of those moving out are part of the same household as children, but the households themselv