      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about logical reasoning, and I need to figure out which assumption is the basis for the argument given. The context is that native speakers perceive sentences as separate words, but actually, it's an illusion, shown by travelers not knowing a language hearing unintelligible streams. The question is asking which option is the assumption the argument depends on.\n\nFirst, let me parse the argument structure. The main point is that when you don't know a language, you might not realize that the sounds you're hearing are just a stream of words, but in reality, the native speakers think they are separate. The example given is travelers not knowing a local language hearing \"unintelligible, uninterrupted stream of sound\" instead of sentences. So the argument is probably about how language is structured and how different people's perception works.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) says that native speakers' perception isn't more accurate than non-native speakers. So the assumption here might be that both groups have similar perception issues, but the argument is presented from a perspective where the problem is with non-native speakers. Wait, the argument is presented as a fact shown by the example. So maybe the assumption is that the problem is that people (maybe non-native speakers) can't distinguish words in their native language? Hmm, not sure yet.\n\nB) says that accomplished non-native speakers don't perceive sentences as streams. But the argument is about non-native speakers hearing streams. If B is true, then the argument might be using non-native speakers as a contrast to native speakers. But the argument is presented as a general fact, so maybe B is part of the assumption. Wait, the question is asking which assumption the argument depends on. The argument is saying that because of this perception issue, travelers (non-native speakers?) hear streams. So maybe the assumption is that non-native speakers can't parse their own language into sentences? But the option B is about accomplished non-native speakers not perceiving sentences as streams. If that's true, then the argument is using non-native speakers as a group that can't parse, but maybe the assumption is that all non-native speakers can't. But the options are separate.\n\nC) says that you can't understand sentences if they're uninterrupted streams. But the argument is about the opposite: when you can't understand, it's because it's a stream. So maybe C is part of the argument. If you can't parse, it's because it's a stream, so C is a consequence, not an assumption. The argument is presented as a fact, so maybe C is the result, not the assumption.\n\nD) says people pay less attention to their own language than foreign languages. The argument is about perception, so maybe D is an assumption. If people don't pay attention to their own language, they might not notice it's a stream. But is that the assumption? The argument is about the existence of the illusion, so maybe D is part of the reason why people can't tell. But I need to think about the structure.\n\nLet me think again. The argument is that because of the illusion, travelers (non-native speakers?) hear streams. So the assumption must be about why non-native speakers can't parse their language. The example is that when you don't know a language, you can't tell the words from the sound. So the assumption would be that the problem is with the perception, not the language itself. So the argument is using the example to show that the illusion exists, which relies on the assumption that people can't parse language when it's a stream. So the assumption is that when you have a continuous stream of sound, you can't distinguish the words. That's option C. But wait, option C says \"It is impossible to understand sentences if they are in fact uninterrupted streams of sound.\" So that's the result. The argument is presented as a fact, so maybe the assumption is that this is