ght be lower. \n\nBut how does this relate to the options? The options are about which statement would resolve the paradox. The paradox is that despite TV having a larger audience, the internet brings in more revenue. So, what explains this? The key is that the cost per ad is higher for TV, so even with a larger audience, the number of ads is smaller, leading to lower total revenue. \n\nSo, to resolve the paradox, the firm must have a better targeting (option D) so that they can reach the same audience with fewer ads, but since the cost per ad is higher, the total revenue is lower. Wait, but the problem states that the campaigns are comparable in size, meaning the same budget is allocated. So, if they can target better (i.e., more accurate targeting), they might get more revenue per ad spend. But the options are about which statement would resolve the paradox. \n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) If they are identical, but the cost per ad is higher for TV, then even with the same audience size, the cost would be higher, leading to lower revenue. But the problem states the campaigns are comparable in size, so the same budget. If they are identical, the number of ads would be the same, but the TV would require fewer ads because the audience is larger. Wait, no. If the campaigns are identical in size, meaning same budget, then the number of ads would be different. For example, if the budget is split between TV and internet, but the total is the same. But the problem says the campaigns are comparable in size, so the same budget. \n\nWait, maybe the key is that the cost per ad is different. If the firm pays more for TV ads (option C), then even though the TV audience is larger, the number of ads would be fewer, leading to lower revenue. But the problem states that the total revenue from TV is 1/3 of the total. If they pay more for TV, they can't spend as much on TV, leading to lower revenue. But option C says they pay more for internet, which would allow them to spend more 