      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's try to figure out this logic problem. So, the context is about a principle and then an application. The principle says that if someone doesn't criticize a certain form of behavior in themselves or vows to stop it, then they shouldn't criticize that form in others. The question is asking which of the options A to D would justify applying this principle to justify the application.\n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the principle correctly. The principle is about not criticizing a form of behavior in others unless you either criticize it in yourself or make a vow to stop it. So, if you don't do either of those things, you can't criticize it in others. The application given is that Shimada doesn't criticize McFeney for being tardy, so according to the principle, that's allowed.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options, if true, would justify why the application of the principle is correct. So, we need to find an option where the facts presented actually fit the principle's logic. That is, if the option is true, then the application of the principle to justify it is valid.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Shimada criticizes McFeney for regularly being tardy, but also criticizes himself for occasional tardiness. Hmm. So, if the principle is that you can't criticize others' behavior unless you criticize it in yourself or make a vow, then if Shimada is criticizing both, that would be allowed. Because he's criticizing in himself (the occasional tardiness) and in others (regularly tardy). So, according to the principle, since he's criticizing it in others when he's also doing it in himself, that's okay. So this seems like a possible justification. But let me check the other options to be sure.\n\nOption B: Neither mcfeney nor shimada is regularly tardy, but Shimada still criticizes McFeney for tardiness. Wait, if neither is regularly tardy, then according to the principle, you can't criticize others'