      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about Innovair reducing ground collisions by training their ground crews. The context says they're spending $500,000 annually on this training. Even though it might cut collisions by half at best, the repair costs drop significantly. The question is to choose the best option that completes the argument.\n\nThe given information is that in a typical year, they have 35 collisions involving ground issues, costing $1,000,000 in repairs. So the argument is probably about how the additional training will save more money because it reduces the number of costly collisions.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Says the least experienced crew cause most collisions. But the argument is about training reducing collisions, so maybe pointing out that experienced crew are involved in more? Not sure yet.\n\nB) Says collisions happen when there are no passengers. But the number of collisions is 35 regardless of passengers. Maybe irrelevant.\n\nC) Says the additional training will focus on avoiding the most costly collisions. That makes sense. If they train to avoid the collisions that cause the most damage, then the reduction in repair costs would be greater. So this would support the argument.\n\nD) Talks about wages during training. The cost is $500,000, but does that include wages? The question is about the argument's logic, not the feasibility. The argument is about reducing collisions leading to lower repair costs, so D might be a distractor. The cost is already given as $500,000, so D's point might not be directly related.\n\nThe key is that the additional training is expected to reduce the number of ground collisions by about half. The repair costs drop \"much greater\". So the argument is probably causative: training reduces collisions, which causes repair costs to drop. The options need to support this.\n\nOption C directly addresses why the reduction in repair costs would be greater: because the additional training will focus on avoiding the most costly collisions. That would maximize the impact, hence the repair costs drop much more. So C is the best completion.\n\nA and D might be there to test if we notice something else, but the main point is the effectiveness of the training leading to greater cost savings. So C is the logical choice.\n</think>\n\nThe argument presented emphasizes that additional training for ground crew will reduce ground collisions by about half, thereby significantly lowering repair costs. The correct completion of the argument must explain why this reduction in repair costs is \"much greater.\" \n\nOption **C** directly addresses this: by focusing the training on avoiding the most costly collisions, the strategy maximizes efficiency. This targeted approach ensures that each reduction in collisions directly lowers repair expenses, aligning with the argument's logic. \n\nOther options either address unrelated issues (e.g., A) or unrelated costs (e.g., D), which do not support the causal link between training and reduced repair costs. \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{C}",
