\n\n- **Statement A is false**: There are no books in some drawers. So there exists at least one drawer without books. But B is supposed to be false. If B is false, then there are books in all drawers. So if B is false, all drawers have books. But A is false, meaning there are no books in some drawers. That's a contradiction. If all drawers have books (from B being false), but A is false, which says there are no books in some drawers. So that's impossible. Therefore, if D is true, then both B and A would have to be true, which is not allowed. Therefore, D cannot be the true statement. So we can eliminate option D as a possibility.\n\nSo summarizing the cases:\n\n- Case 1 (A true): Contradiction because B would also be true.\n- Case 2 (B true): C is true, others false. Options C must be true here.\n- Case 3 (C true): Contradiction because A and B would be true.\n- Case 4 (D true): Contradiction because B and A would be true.\n\nTherefore, the only possible scenario where exactly one statement is true is when B is true, leading to C being true. Therefore, the correct answer must be option C.\n\nBut wait, let me double-check. If B is true (all drawers have books), then C is false (there is a pen in the second drawer). So the second drawer has no pen. D is false (no letters in third drawer). A is false (there are books in some drawers, but since all have books, A's statement that \"there are no books in some drawers\" is false. So all statements except B are false. Then, looking at the options:\n\n- A: pen in second drawer. But C is false, so no pen there. So A is false.\n- B: letters in third drawer. D is false, so no letters there. So B is false.\n- C: books in all four drawers. B is true, so this is true.\n- D: no books in all four drawers. But B is true, so this is false.\n\nTherefore, only C is true. So the answer must be C.\n\nBut wait, the options given are A, B, C, D. The question is asking \"which of the following must be true?\" So if in the only possible scen