rt Zhang Qiang's argument that price improvement leads to consumption increase, thereby weakening Li Ming's rebuttal. So A could be a candidate.\n\nOption B: Even if the economy continues to deteriorate, consumers still believe the situation will improve. If consumers have hope that the economy will get better, they might not increase consumption now because they think later prices will be lower. This is a bit about consumer behavior in a depressed economy. If the economy is getting worse, but people still expect it to improve, they might not spend as much. But how does this affect Li Ming's argument? Li Ming's argument is that even if prices are low, consumption doesn't increase because people don't have jobs. If consumers still expect the economy to improve, maybe they don't increase consumption now. But if the economy is actually improving (as per Zhang Qiang's argument), then maybe they would. Wait, but the question is about which option weakens Li Ming's rebuttal. If the option says that even when prices hit bottom, people don't increase consumption because they think the economy will get worse, that would support Li Ming's argument. But if the option says that even in a depressed economy, consumers still try to spend now, that would contradict Li Ming's argument. But option B says even if the economy continues to deteriorate, consumers still believe the situation will improve. So that would mean they don't increase consumption now, which supports Li Ming's idea that consumption doesn't go up when prices are low. So B might actually support Li Ming's argument, so it's not the answer.\n\nOption C: Even in extreme economic downturn, the unemployed must continue buying necessities. So if the economy is really bad, the unemployed have to buy food and other basics. But Li Ming's argument is that unemployed can't increase consumption because they lose jobs. But if even when unemployed, they have to buy necessities, maybe they can't save money for other things. Wait, 