adict Li Ming's argument. So D is more likely to weaken the rebuttal.\n\nWait, but let me check again. Li Ming's argument is that when prices hit rock bottom, even though they are low, people can't increase consumption because they lose jobs. But if the economy is about to improve, companies hire more, so people have jobs. Then, even if prices are low, they can spend. So if A is true, that when the economy improves, people buy postponed goods, which would mean consumption increases. Therefore, A supports Zhang Qiang's argument. So A is not the answer. D, on the other hand, says that even when prices are low, people will save instead of spend. If that's true, then even with low prices, consumption doesn't increase because they have jobs but don't want to spend. That directly contradicts Li Ming's argument. Therefore, D would weaken the rebuttal.\n\nSo the answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nTo determine which option weakens Li Ming's rebuttal, we analyze the arguments:\n\n1. **Zhang Qiang** posits that lower prices lead to increased consumption, improving the economy.  \n2. **Li Ming** disagrees, arguing that even with low prices, job losses prevent consumption growth.  \n\n**Option Evaluation**:  \n- **A**: If the economy improves, people buy postponed goods. This supports Zhang Qiang\u2019s argument (consumption increases with jobs), so **A does not weaken the rebuttal**.  \n- **B**: Consumers still believe the economy will improve even in a depressed phase. This suggests they won\u2019t increase consumption now, aligning with Li Ming\u2019s position. **B supports the rebuttal**, not weakening it.  \n- **C**: Even in extreme downturns, people must buy necessities. If they have jobs (e.g., as the economy improves), they can spend, reinforcing Zhang Qiang\u2019s logic. **C supports the rebuttal**, not weakening.  \n- **D**: In economic downturns, non-employed people reduce spending. When prices hit rock bottom, even with jobs, people might prefer saving instead of spending. This directly contradicts Li Ming\u2019s claim that low prices spur consumption. **D weakens the rebuttal** by illustrating that even with jobs, consumption doesn\u2019t rise.  \n\n**Conclusion**: Option **D** most weakens Li Ming\u2019s rebuttal.  \n\n\\boxed{D}",
