      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's try to figure out this logic problem. So, the context given is about whether absolute fairness leads to distribution according to work, and vice versa. The statements are:\n\n1. If absolute fairness is implemented, it will inevitably lead to distribution according to work.\n2. If distribution according to work is implemented, there will be inequality between the rich and the poor.\n3. Only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options A to D is correct based on these statements. The options are:\n\nA) Distribution according to work must be implemented\nB) Distribution on demand must be implemented\nC) Absolute fairness must be implemented\nD) Can't implement absolute fairness\n\nHmm. Let me break this down. First, let's parse each statement.\n\nStatement 1: Absolute fairness \u2192 Distribution according to work. So, if we do absolute fairness, then distribution must be according to work. That's a conditional.\n\nStatement 2: Distribution according to work \u2192 Inequality between rich and poor. Another conditional. So doing distribution according to work leads to inequality.\n\nStatement 3: Only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented. This is a bit trickier. The structure here is \"Only by X can Y be achieved.\" Which usually translates to \"If Y is achieved, then X must have been done.\" So, if absolute fairness is implemented (Y), then equalizing the rich and the poor (X) must have happened. So, Y \u2192 X.\n\nNow, the question is about what can or must be implemented. The options are about distribution according to work, distribution on demand, absolute fairness, or inability to implement absolute fairness.\n\nLet me try to model this with logical implications.\n\nFrom statement 1: A \u2192 B, where A is absolute fairness, B is distribution according to work.\n\nFrom statement 2: B \u2192 C, where C is inequality between rich and poor.\n\nFrom statement 3: C \u2192 A, because \"Only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented.\" So, if you have absolute fairness (A), then you must have equalized the rich and the poor (C). Therefore, C is a necessary consequence of A.\n\nWait, but statement 3 is phrased as \"Only by X can Y be achieved.\" In logic terms, that's equivalent to \"If Y is true, then X must be true.\" So, Y \u2192 X. So, if we have A (absolute fairness), then we must have C (equalizing). Therefore, A \u2192 C.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\nA \u2192 B (Statement 1)\nB \u2192 C (Statement 2)\nA \u2192 C (Statement 3)\n\nWait, but if A \u2192 C and C is equivalent to B via the second statement (since B leads to C), then A \u2192 C is the same as A \u2192 B. So, maybe statement 3 is redundant? Or perhaps there's a cycle here.\n\nWait, let's see. If A is true, then B must be true (from A \u2192 B). Then, since B is true, C must be true (from B \u2192 C). Therefore, if A is true, then C must be true. So, A \u2192 C. But also, since C is true (if we have equality), then B must be true (from C \u2192 B). Wait, no. Wait, statement 3 is C \u2192 A, not C \u2192 B. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, statement 3 is: Only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented. So, in other words, to have absolute fairness (A), you must have equalized the rich and the poor (C). So, A \u2192 C.\n\nBut statement 2 is B \u2192 C. So, if you have distribution according to work (B), then you have inequality (C). But if you have A (absolute fairness), you must have C (equalizing). So, A \u2192 C, and C is a necessary condition for A. But how does this relate to B?\n\nWait, maybe we can chain these implications. Let's see:\n\nFrom statement 1: A \u2192 B\n\nFrom statement 3: A \u2192 C\n\nBut statement 2 says B \u2192 C. So, if you have B, you must have C. But if you have A, you must have C. So, if A is true, then C is true. If C is true, then B could be either true or false? Wait, no. Because statement 2 says if B is true, then C is true. But if C is true, does that mean B must be true? Not necessarily. Unless C is equivalent to B. But according to statement 2, B \u2192 C, but C can be true without B being true. For example, if you have some other condition that leads to C. Wait, but in the context of the problem, maybe all these statements are interconnected.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that the three statements form a cycle? Let me check again.\n\nStatement 1: Absolute fairness leads to distribution according to work.\n\nStatement 2: Distribution according to work leads to inequality.\n\nStatement 3: Inequality implies that absolute fairness cannot be maintained, hence you need to equalize to achieve absolute fairness.\n\nWait, maybe statement 3 is saying that if you have inequality (C), then you cannot have absolute fairness (A), unless you equalize. So, if you have C (inequality), then A (absolute fairness) is not possible unless you have C. Wait, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe statement 3 is a definition. \"Only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented.\" So, in other words, absolute fairness can only be implemented if you equalize the rich and the poor. Therefore, if you have absolute fairness (A), then you must have equalized the rich and the poor (C). So, A \u2192 C.\n\nBut statement 2 says that if you have distribution according to work (B), then you have inequality (C). So, B \u2192 C.\n\nSo, combining these:\n\nIf A is true, then B is true (A \u2192 B), and B is true, so C is true (B \u2192 C). Therefore, A \u2192 C.\n\nBut also, if C is true, does that mean anything about A? Not directly. Unless C is equivalent to A. But according to statement 3, A \u2192 C, but C could be caused by something else. Wait, but in the context of the problem, maybe all these statements are connected. Let me try to see the relationships.\n\nSo, the three statements form a set of implications:\n\n1. A \u2192 B\n\n2. B \u2192 C\n\n3. C \u2192 A\n\nWait, is that possible? If A \u2192 B and B \u2192 C, then by transitivity, A \u2192 C. But statement 3 is C \u2192 A. So, combining these, we get A \u2192 C and C \u2192 A, which would make A \u2194 C. But that would mean absolute fairness is equivalent to equalizing the rich and the poor. But that seems odd.\n\nAlternatively, maybe statement 3 is not C \u2192 A, but rather A \u2194 C. Because \"Only by equalizing... can absolute fairness be implemented.\" So, in order to have absolute fairness, you must equalize, and if you equalize, you have absolute fairness. So, A \u2194 C. But that would mean they are equivalent. But statement 1 says A \u2192 B, and statement 3 would then imply C \u2192 B as well. But statement 2 is B \u2192 C. So, if B \u2192 C and C \u2192 B, then B \u2194 C. But that would mean distribution according to work is equivalent to inequality. Which also seems odd.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misinterpreting statement 3. Let's parse it again: \"Only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented.\" The phrase \"only by\" implies that equalizing is a necessary condition for absolute fairness. So, in logical terms, this is equivalent to \"If absolute fairness is implemented, then the rich and the poor have been equalized.\" So, A \u2192 C. So, if you have A, then you must have C. Therefore, A \u2192 C.\n\nBut statement 2 is B \u2192 C. So, if you have B, you must have C. So, if you have distribution according to work, you must have equalized the rich and the poor. But if you have A, you must have C. So, A \u2192 C and C is a necessary condition for A. But how does that relate to B?\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that the three statements form a cycle. Let's see:\n\nIf A is true, then B must be true (A \u2192 B). If B is true, then C must be true (B \u2192 C). If C is true, then A must be true (C \u2192 A). So, this forms a cycle: A \u219