remise doesn't tell us anything. However, the option says: If revising it does not affect its popularity with cadres (i.e., C is false), then we should revise immediately. But why would revising affect the popularity with cadres? The premises don't link R (revising) to C (not being popular with cadres). So, if we decide to revise, does that affect C? Not necessarily. The premise is that if C is true, then R must be true. But the option is saying that if R is false (i.e., we don't revise), then we should not do something. Wait, the option is phrased as: If the revision does not affect its popularity with cadres, then we should revise immediately. Wait, \"if the revision... will not affect its popularity with cadres\"\u2014so if C is false (i.e., the measure is still popular with cadres), then we should revise immediately. But why would revising affect C? The premise is C \u2192 R, but not R \u2192 C. So, if we don't revise (R is false), then C could still be true or false. The option is saying that if R is false (i.e., we don't revise), then we should revise immediately. But that doesn't follow. The premises don't link R to C. So, even if we don't revise (R is false), the measure could still be not popular with cadres (C is false), but there's no necessity to do anything about it. Therefore, the option C is not necessarily true. Unless there's an implicit assumption that if you don't revise, you have to do something else, but the premises don't specify that. So, maybe option C is not necessarily true.\n\nOption D: If this reform measure is Welcome by the cadres, we should adopt a new reform measure.\n\nSo, if C is true (the measure is welcomed by cadres), then we should adopt a new measure (N). Let's check the premises. Premise 1: If C, then R (revise). Premise 2: If W, then N (adopt new measure). So, if C is true, then according to premise 1, we should revise. But option D says if C is true, we should adopt a new measure. That contradicts premise 1. Therefore, option D is not true because if the measure is welcomed by cadres (C is true), we must revise, not adopt a new measure. So, option D is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The original problem states: \"If this reform measure is not Welcome by the cadres, we should revise it. If it is not popular with workers, we should adopt a new reform measure.\" So, if the measure is not welcomed by cadres (C), revise. If not popular with workers (W), adopt new measure. So, if C is true (welcomed by cadres), then according to premise 1, we must revise. Therefore, option D says if it's Welcome by cadres (C is true), we should adopt a new measure. But according to the premises, we should revise. Therefore, option D is incorrect.\n\nSo, going back to the options:\n\nA: If and only if it does not reduce its reputation among workers, we should revise. But the premises don't link R (revising) to the measure's popularity. So, unless \"reputation\" is being used differently, this seems unrelated.\n\nB: Strive to popularize among cadres or workers. But the premises don't indicate any necessity to make it popular. They only specify conditions under which to revise or adopt a new measure. So, unless there's an implicit assumption that the measure should be acceptable to both groups, but premise 3 says it's not. So, maybe the measure is not supposed to be acceptable to both, but there's no necessity to make it acceptable to either. So, option B might not be true.\n\nC: If revising does not affect popularity with cadres, then revise immediately. But the premises don't link R to C. So, even if we don't revise (R is false), the measure could still be popular with cadres (C is false). The option is saying that if R is false, then we should do something, but the premises don't specify what to do if R is false. The premises only tell us what to do when C or W is true. So, if R is false, we don't have any premise that tells us what to do. Therefore, the option C is not necessarily true.\n\nD: If the measure is Welcome by cadres (C is true), adopt new measure. But premise 1 says if C is true, we should revise. So, D is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the only remaining option is none of them? But that can't be. Wait, maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, let's check option A again. The option says: \"We should revise this reform measure if and only if it does not reduce its reputation among workers.\"\n\nBut the premises are about the measure not being popular with one of the groups. The measure's popularity with workers is W. If W is true, we adopt a new measure. If C is true, we revise. But how does \"reputation among workers\" come into play here?\n\nWait, maybe \"reputation\" here refers to the measure's acceptance among the public, particularly workers. If the measure is not popular with workers (W), then its \"reputation\" (popularity) among workers is low, which would lead to the conclusion that we should adopt a new measure. But option A is saying that we should revise only if the measure does not reduce its \"reputation\" among workers. But the premises don't link R (revising) to the measure's popularity. So, unless \"reputation\" is being used in a different way, perhaps as the measure's acceptance or the possibility of it being revised. But I'm not sure. Maybe the problem is in the translation or wording.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"reputation\" here is a mistranslation or misphrasing. If the original problem is in Chinese, maybe \"reputation\" is a translation of a term that actually refers to something else. Alternatively, maybe \"reputation\" here is meant to refer to the measure's acceptance among the groups mentioned. If the measure is not popular with workers (W), then its \"reputation\" (popularity) among workers is low, which would trigger the need to adopt a new measure. But option A is saying that we should revise only if the measure does not reduce its \"reputation\" among workers. But the premises don't specify that reducing \"reputation\" leads to r