escendants not being subjects of Zhou. For example, maybe all Guzhu descendants are subjects of Zhou, except for those mentioned in the second statement. But the second statement only says that some subjects of Zhou are not Guzhu descendants. It doesn't say anything about Guzhu descendants in general. So, it's possible that all Guzhu descendants are subjects of Zhou, except for some. But we can't conclude that some Guzhu descendants are not subjects of Zhou. Therefore, Option D might not be correct.\n\nSo, summarizing:\n\n- Option A: Unclear, since we don't know about Wei gatherers' relation to Guzhu.\n- Option B: Unclear, since we don't know about Wei gatherers' relation to Guzhu.\n- Option C: Correct, because subjects of Zhou can't be Wei gatherers.\n- Option D: Unclear, because the second statement doesn't negate all Guzhu descendants from being subjects of Zhou.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be Option C.\n</think>\n\nThe problem involves logical deductions based on two statements:\n\n1. **All Wei gatherers were not the subjects of the Western Zhou Dynasty.**  \n   This establishes a **disjoint set** between Wei gatherers and Zhou subjects. No overlap exists between these groups.\n\n2. **Some subjects of the Western Zhou Dynasty were not the descendants of Guzhu.**  \n   This indicates that not all Zhou subjects claim descent from Guzhu, but it does **not** assert anything about Wei gatherers specifically.\n\n**Analysis of Options:**  \n- **A) Some Wei gatherers are descendants of Guzhu**  \n  The first statement excludes Wei gatherers from Zhou subjects, but it does not address their relation to Guzhu descendants. This conclusion is uncertain.  \n\n- **B) Some Wei pickers are not descendants of Guzhu**  \n  The term \"Wei pickers\" is likely a mistranslation. Even if Wei gatherers are distinct from Zhou subjects, there is no information linking them to Guzhu descendants. This remains unknown.  \n\n- **C) All subjects of the Western Zhou dynasty were no