face value because it creates a paradox.\n\nApplying that to this problem: If every general rule has at least one exception (Lance's conclusion), then assuming that this is a general rule (i.e., a universal statement) leads to the conclusion that there's a general rule without exceptions. But that contradicts the original premise. Therefore, Frank is pointing out that accepting Lance's conclusion leads to a contradiction, so the conclusion must be invalid.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Demonstrating that Lance assumes the very thing he sets out to prove. Hmm. So, if Frank is showing that by asserting the general rule (that every general rule has an exception), he is assuming the thing he's trying to prove? That might be a form of circular reasoning or assuming the conclusion to prove it. But I need to check.\n\nB) Establishing that experience teaches us the opposite of what Lance concludes. Wait, experience teaches that every general rule has an exception. But Frank is not saying that experience contradicts that. He's saying that if we take that as a general rule, then it leads to a contradiction. So B might not be correct.\n\nC) Showing that Lance's conclusion involves him in a contradiction. Yes, exactly. If we take his conclusion as a general rule (which would be \"Every general rule has an exception\"), then that statement itself is a general rule with no exceptions. Therefore, his conclusion leads to a contradiction because he's asserting that all general rules have exceptions, but then the statement \"Every general rule has an exception\" is a general rule with no exceptions. So that's a contradiction. Therefore, C seems correct.\n\nD) Showing that it has no implications for any real cases. But Frank's argument does have implications for real cases. The implication is that the original conclusion (Lance's) is invalid because it leads to a paradox. So D might not be correct.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The options are about what Frank's argument is attempt