nce classes than liberal arts classes. But does that mean that each science class has more boys than girls? Again, not necessarily. The total number of boys in all science classes could be more than the total number of girls in all science classes, but individual classes could have more girls. For example, if there are two science classes, one with 3 boys and 2 girls, and another with 2 boys and 3 girls, the total boys would be 5 and total girls 5. But the problem says there are more science classes than liberal arts classes. Wait, but in this case, the number of science classes could be more, but individual classes might not have more boys than girls. So, Option B might not hold either. Hmm.\n\nOption C: The total number of boys in the liberal arts class is higher than that of girls in the science class. Hmm. This is a bit more complex. So, comparing the total boys in liberal arts classes to the total girls in science classes. Let's think. We know that overall, boys > girls. Also, there are more science classes than liberal arts classes. Let me try to construct a possible scenario. Suppose there are two liberal arts classes and three science classes. Let's say each liberal arts class has 10 students, all boys. So total boys in liberal arts would be 20. Each science class has 10 students, with 6 boys and 4 girls. So total boys in science would be 30, and total girls in science would be 12. Then, total boys overall would be 20 + 30 = 50, and total girls would be 12 + 12 = 24. So boys (50) > girls (24), which satisfies the first condition. Now, total boys in liberal arts (20) vs total girls in science (12). Here, 20 > 12, so Option C holds. But is this always the case?\n\nWait, let's try another scenario. Suppose there are two liberal arts classes and three science classes. Let's say liberal arts classes have 5 boys and 5 girls each. So total boys in liberal arts would be 10, total girls 10. Science classes: each science class has 5 boys and 5 girls. So total boys in 