rrect.\n\nWait, but in this case, the number of science classes is more than liberal arts classes. Let's check: two liberal arts classes, three science classes. Yes. So in this scenario, Option C is false. Therefore, Option C is not necessarily correct.\n\nMoving on to Option D: The total number of boys in the science class is more than that of girls in the liberal arts class. So, total boys in science vs total girls in liberal arts. Let's test this with previous examples.\n\nFirst example: liberal arts classes had 10 boys and 10 girls (total 20), science classes had 30 boys and 12 girls (total 30). Total boys: 50, girls: 24. Total boys in science (30) vs total girls in liberal arts (10). 30 > 10, so Option D holds here.\n\nAnother example: liberal arts classes had 6 boys and 4 girls (total 10), science classes 5 boys and 3 girls (total 8). Total boys: 16, girls: 13. Total boys in science (5) vs total girls in liberal arts (4). 5 > 4, so D holds.\n\nAnother example where D might fail: suppose liberal arts classes have 3 boys and 7 girls (total 10), science classes 7 boys and 3 girls (total 10). Total boys: 10, girls: 10. But this doesn't satisfy the condition that boys > girls. So invalid.\n\nAnother case: liberal arts classes 5 boys and 5 girls (total 10), science classes 6 boys and 4 girls (total 10). Total boys: 11, girls: 9. Here, total boys in science (6) vs total girls in liberal arts (5). 6 > 5, so D holds.\n\nWait, is there a scenario where total boys in science is less than total girls in liberal arts?\n\nLet me try. Suppose liberal arts classes have 1 boy and 9 girls (total 10), science classes have 1 boy and 9 girls (total 10). But total boys would be 2, girls 18. Doesn't satisfy boys > girls. Not valid.\n\nAnother attempt: liberal arts classes: 2 boys and 8 girls (total 10), science classes: 1 boy and 9 girls (total 10). Total boys: 3, girls: 17. Still boys < girls. Not valid.\n\nWait, maybe if liberal arts classes have more girls, but science classes have more boys. Let's see. Suppose liberal arts classes: 4 boys and 6 girls (total 10), science classes: 7 boys and 3 girls (total 10). Total boys: 11, girls: 15. Boys < girls. Not valid.\n\nAlternatively, liberal arts classes: 3 boys and 7 girls (total 10), science classes: 6 boys and 4 girls (total 10). Total boys: 9, girls: 11. Still boys < girls. Not valid.\n\nHmm, maybe it's impossible for total boys in science to be less than total girls in liberal arts? Let's think.\n\nSuppose we have L liberal arts classes and S science classes, with S > L.\n\nTotal boys: B = sum of boys in all classes.\n\nTotal girls: G = sum of girls in all classes.\n\nGiven that B > G.\n\nWe need to see if B_S (total boys in science) > G_L (total girls in liberal arts).\n\nIs this necessarily true?\n\nLet me try to set up equations.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nFor liberal arts classes: each has L1, L2, ..., L_L classes.\n\nEach class has B_Li boys and G_Li girls.\n\nTotal boys in liberal arts: sum_{i=1 to L} B_Li\n\nTotal girls in liberal arts: sum_{i=1 to L} G_Li\n\nSimilarly, for science classes: S1, S2, ..., S_S classes.\n\nTotal boys in science: sum_{j=1 to S} B_Sj\n\nTotal girls in science: sum_{j=1 to S} G_Sj\n\nGiven that sum_{i=1 to L} B_Li + sum_{j=1 to S} B_Sj > sum_{i=1 to L} G_Li + sum_{j=1 to S} G_Sj\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\nsum_{i=1 to L} (B_Li - G_Li) + sum_{j=1 to S} (B_Sj - G_Sj) > 0\n\nBut we need to see if sum_{j=1 to S} (B_Sj - G_Sj) > sum_{i=1 to L} (G_Li - B_Li)\n\nWait, because if we rearrange the inequality:\n\nsum_{j=1 to S} (B_Sj - G_Sj) > sum_{i=1 to L} (G_Li - B_Li)\n\nBut since sum_{j=1 to S} (B_Sj - G_Sj) + sum_{i=1 to L} (B_Li - G_Li) > 0\n\nBut the question is whether sum_{j=1 to S} (B_Sj - G_Sj) > sum_{i=1 to L} (G_Li - B_Li)\n\nIs this necessarily true?\n\nAlternatively, can we have sum_{j=1 to S} (B_Sj - G_Sj) <= sum_{i=1 to L} (G_Li - B_Li) while still having total boys > total girls?\n\nLet me try to construct a case where this happens.\n\nSuppose we have L = 2 liberal arts classes, S = 3 science classes.\n\nLet\u2019s assign:\n\nFor liberal arts classes:\n\nClass 1: B1 = 1, G1 = 2 (so B1 - G1 = -1)\n\nClass 2: B2 = 1, G2 = 2 (B2 - G2 = -1)\n\nTotal for liberal arts: B_total = 2, G_total = 4\n\nFor science classes:\n\nClass 1: B3 = 1, G3 = 1 (B3 - G3 = 0)\n\nClass 2: B4 = 1, G4 = 1 (B4 - G4 = 0)\n\nClass 3: B5 = 1, G5 = 1 (B5 - G5 = 0)\n\nTotal for science: B_total = 3, G_total = 3\n\nTotal boys overall: 2 + 3 = 5\n\nTotal girls overall: 4 + 3 = 7\n\nBut here, boys (5) < girls (7), which violates the given condition. So this is invalid.\n\nAnother attempt. Let's have liberal arts classes with more girls, but science classes with more boys.\n\nL = 2, S = 3.\n\nLiberal arts classes:\n\nClass 1: B1 = 2, G1 = 3 (B1 - G1 = -1)\n\nClass 2: B2 = 2, G2 = 3 (B2 - G2 = -1)\n\nTotal liberal arts: B_total = 4, G_total = 6\n\nScience classes:\n\nClass 1: B3 = 4, G3 = 0 (B3 - G3 = 4)\n\nClass 2: B4 = 4, G4 = 0 (B4 - G4 = 4)\n\nClass 3: B5 = 4, G5 = 0 (B5 - G5 = 4)\n\nTotal science: B_total = 12, G_total = 0\n\nTotal boys overall: 4 + 12 = 16\n\nTotal girls overall: 6 + 0 = 6\n\nHere, boys (16) > girls (6), which satisfies the condition. Now, check Option D: total boys in science (12) vs total girls in liberal arts (6). 12 > 6, so D holds.\n\nAnother case. Let's try to make total boys in science less than total girls in liberal arts.\n\nSuppose L = 2, S = 3.\n\nLiberal arts classes:\n\nClass 1: B1 = 3, G1 = 4 (B1 - G1 = -1)\n\nClass 2: B2 = 3, G2 = 4 (B2 - G2 = -1)\n\nTotal liberal arts: B_total = 6, G_total = 8\n\nScience classes:\n\nClass 1: B3 = 1, G3 = 0 (B3 - G3 = 1)\n\nClass 2: B4 = 1, G4 = 0 (B4 - G4 = 1)\n\nClass 3: B5 = 1, G5 = 0 (B5 - G5 = 1)\n\nTotal science: B_total = 3, G_total = 0\n\nTotal boys overall: 6 + 3 = 9\n\nTotal girls overall: 8 + 0 = 8\n\nHere, boys (9) > girls (8), which is okay. Now, check Option D: total boys in science (3) vs total girls in liberal arts (8). 3 < 8, so D does not hold here. But wait, this contradicts our previous assumption. Wait, but in this case, total boys in science (3) is less than total girls in liberal arts (8). But according to the problem's conditions, is this possible?\n\nWait, but in this case, the total boys in science (3) is less than total girls in liberal arts (8). But according to the problem's conditions, is there a way this can happen? Let's check the numbers again.\n\nTotal boys overall: 9, total girls: 8. So boys > girls. The number of science classes (3) is more than liberal arts classes (2). So according to this, Option D is not necessarily true. But wait, in this case, the answer would be that Option D is not necessarily correct. But earlier examples showed that D was correct. So is there a case where D is false?\n\nWait, in this example, the total boys in science (3) is less than total girls in liberal arts (8). But according to the problem's conditions, is this possible? Let's check if the total boys and girls add up correctly.\n\nTotal boys: 3 (science) + 6 (liberal arts) = 9\n\nTotal girls: 0 (science) + 8 (liberal arts) = 8\n\nYes, boys (9) > girls (8). So this is a valid scenario. Therefore, in this case, Option D is false. Therefore, Option D is not necessarily correct.\n\nBut wait, this contradicts my previous examples where D was true. So in some cases, D is true, in others, it's false. Therefore, D is not necessarily correct.\n\nWait, but in this case, the number of science classes is more than liberal arts classes, but the total boys in science can be less than total girls in liberal arts. So Option D is not necessarily correct.\n\nBut wait, in this example, the total boys in science (3) is less than total girls in liberal arts (8). So D is false here. Therefore, D is not necessarily correct.\n\nBut earlier, in other scenarios, D was true. So D is not necessarily correct.\n\nWait, but in this case, the total number of boys in science is 3, and total girls in liberal arts is 8. So 3 < 8. So D is false here. Therefore, D is not necessarily correct.\n\nBut then, in this case, the answer would be that none of the options must be correct? But that can't be. Wait, let's check again.\n\nWait, in this example, the total boys in science (3) is less than total girls in liberal arts (8). So D is false here. Therefore, D is not necessarily correct.\n\nBut in other scenarios, D is true. So D is not necessarily correct.\n\nBut then, in the previous example where D was true, but in another example, D is false. Therefore, D is not necessarily correct.\n\nBut wait, the problem states that there are more science classes than liberal arts classes. So in the example where D is false, the number of science classes is still more. So the answer is that none of the options must be correct? But that seems odd.\n\nWait, let's check the options again.\n\nOption A: Total boys in liberal arts > total girls in liberal arts. In the first example, this was true (20 vs 20). In the second example, with liberal arts classes having 10 boys and 10 girls, so A is true. But in another example where liberal arts classes had 10 boys and 10 girls, A is true. Wait, but in the example where liberal arts classes had 10 boys and 10 girls, total boys in liberal arts is equal to total girls in liberal arts. So A is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but in the example where liberal arts classes had 10 boys and 10 girls, total boys in liberal arts is equal to total girls in liberal arts. So A is not necessarily true. So A can be true or false.\n\nSimilarly, for Option B: total boys in science > total girls in science. In the first example, total boys in science (30) > total girls in science (12). In another example, where science classes had 5 boys and 3 girls each, total boys in science (15) > total girls in science (9). But in the example where science classes had 3 boys and 6 girls each, total boys in science (9) vs total girls in science (18). Here, 9 < 18, so B is false. So B is not necessarily correct.\n\nOption C: total boys in liberal arts > total girls in science. In the first example, 20 vs 12, which is true. In another example where liberal arts classes had 10 boys and 10 girls, and science classes had 5 boys and 3 girls, total boys in liberal arts (10) vs total girls in science (9). 10 > 9, so C is true. But in the example where liberal arts classes had 10 boys and 10 girls, and science classes had 3 boys and 6 girls, total boys in liberal arts (10) vs total girls in science (6). 10 > 6, so C is true. Wait, but earlier, when I tried to create a case where C is false, I couldn't. Wait, in the previous example where liberal arts classes had 10 boys and 10 girls, and science classes had 3 boys and 6 girls, total boys in liberal arts (10) vs total girls in science (6). 10 > 6. So C is true. Wait, maybe I made a mistake earlier.\n\nWait, in the example where liberal arts classes had 10 boys and 10 girls (total 20), science classes had 3 boys and 6 girls (total 9). Total boys overall 29, girls 16. So boys > girls. Then, total boys in liberal arts (10) vs total girls in science (6). 10 > 6. So C is true. So maybe C is always true?\n\nWait, let's think again. Let's suppose that there are L liberal arts classes and S science classes, with S > L.\n\nTotal boys: B = sum_{i=1 to L} B_i + sum_{j=1 to S} B_j\n\nTotal girls: G = sum_{i=1 to L} G_i + sum_{j=1 to S} G_j\n\nGiven that B > G.\n\nWe need to check if sum_{i=1 to L} B_i > sum_{j=1 to S} G_j.\n\nIs this necessarily true?\n\nLet me try to use the given conditions. Since S > L, and B > G.\n\nBut how to relate these.\n\nAlternatively, consider that in each liberal arts class, the number of boys is at least the number of girls? Not necessarily. But overall, the total boys exceed total girls.\n\nBut perhaps we can use the pigeonhole principle. If there are more science classes than liberal arts classes, then perhaps the total boys in science must exceed the total girls in liberal arts.\n\nWait, let's think of it as:\n\nTotal boys in science (B_S) and total girls in liberal arts (G_L). We need to see if B_S > G_L.\n\nBut we know that total boys (B) > total girls (G). So B = B_L + B_S\n\nG = G_L + G_S\n\nGiven that B > G, so B_L + B_S > G_L + G_S\n\nBut we need to relate B_S and G_L.\n\nIs there a way to derive B_S > G_L from the given conditions?\n\nAlternatively, rearrange the inequality:\n\nB_L - G_L > B_S - G_S\n\nBut not sure.\n\nAlternatively, think of the difference between boys and girls in each class.\n\nFor liberal arts classes: sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > 0\n\nFor science classes: sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) > 0\n\nBut the total difference is sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) + sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) > 0\n\nBut we need to see if sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) > sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i)\n\nWait, because:\n\nsum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) > sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i)\n\nIs equivalent to:\n\nsum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) + sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > 0\n\nWhich is the same as the total difference being positive. So this is equivalent to the given condition. Therefore, the inequality sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) > sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i) is equivalent to B > G.\n\nBut we need to see if sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) > sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i)\n\nWhich is equivalent to sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) + sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > 0\n\nBut since sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > 0 (because B > G), then sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) must be positive as well. But does that mean it's greater than sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i)?\n\nWait, not necessarily. For example, if sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) is positive, but sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i) is also positive, but their sum is positive. But individually, they could be such that sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) is less than sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i). Wait, but how?\n\nWait, let's take numbers. Suppose sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 5\n\nsum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) = 3\n\nTotal difference: 8 > 0\n\nBut sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) = 3 < sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i) = 5\n\nBut in this case, total difference is 8, which is still positive. So even if one sum is less than the other, the total is still positive.\n\nTherefore, it's possible for sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) to be less than sum_{i=1 to L} (G_i - B_i), but their sum is still positive. Therefore, the individual sums could be such that one is larger and the other is smaller, but their sum is still positive.\n\nTherefore, Option C: sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j)\n\nWait, no. Option C is sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j)\n\nWait, no. Option C is: total boys in liberal arts (sum B_i) > total girls in science (sum G_j). Which is equivalent to sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j)\n\nBut according to the previous analysis, sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) + sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) > 0\n\nBut this does not necessarily mean that sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j)\n\nFor example, suppose sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 6 and sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) = 2. Then total difference is 8 > 0. But sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 6 > sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j) = 2. So in this case, Option C holds.\n\nAnother example: sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 5, sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) = 3. Total difference 8 > 0. Then sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 5 > sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j) = 2. So C holds.\n\nAnother example: sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 4, sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) = 4. Total difference 8 > 0. Then sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 4 > sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j) = 4? No, they are equal. So in this case, sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j). So Option C would be false here. But wait, in this case, total difference is 8 > 0. So B > G. But sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 4 and sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j) = 4. So total difference is 8. But sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = 4 and sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j) = 4. So sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) = sum_{j=1 to S} (B_j - G_j). Therefore, sum_{i=1 to L} (B_i - G_i) > sum_{j=1 to S} (G_j - B_j) would 