ore girls, then D is true. Wait, no. If science classes have more girls, then total boys in science would be less than total girls in liberal arts. For example, if liberal arts classes have 10 boys and 10 girls, and science classes have 10 girls and 0 boys. Then total boys in science (0) vs total girls in liberal arts (10). So D is false here. But in this case, total boys in science (0) is less than total girls in liberal arts (10). So D is false.\n\nBut in this case, total boys overall would be 10 + 0 = 10, total girls 10 + 10 = 20. So boys < girls. Which violates the problem's condition. Therefore, this scenario is invalid.\n\nTherefore, in valid scenarios where boys > girls, can we have D being true or false?\n\nIf liberal arts classes have more girls, and science classes have more girls, then total boys in science could be less than total girls in liberal arts. But total boys overall must still be greater than total girls.\n\nFor example:\n\nL = 2 liberal arts classes: 5 boys and 5 girls each. Total boys: 10, total girls: 10.\n\nS = 3 science classes: 0 boys and 10 girls each. Total boys: 0, total girls: 30.\n\nTotal boys: 10, total girls: 40. But boys < girls. Invalid.\n\nAnother example:\n\nL = 2 liberal arts classes: 6 boys and 4 girls each. Total boys: 12, total girls: 8.\n\nS = 3 science classes: 3 boys and 6 girls each. Total boys: 9, total girls: 18.\n\nTotal boys: 21, total girls: 26. Boys < girls. Invalid.\n\nAnother example:\n\nL = 2 liberal arts classes: 4 boys and 6 girls each. Total boys: 8, total girls: 12.\n\nS = 3 science classes: 5 boys and 3 girls each. Total boys: 15, total girls: 9.\n\nTotal boys: 23, total girls: 21. Boys > girls. Here, total boys in science (15) vs total girls in liberal arts (12). 15 > 12, so D is true.\n\nAnother example:\n\nL = 2 liberal arts classes: 3 boys and 7 girls each. Total boys: 6, total girls: 14.\n\nS = 3 science classes: 7 boys and 3 girls each. Total boys: 49, total girls: 9.\n\nTotal boys: 55, total girls: 