e active. So A is not necessarily correct.\n\nSo, reviewing all options, the only one that fits is D? Wait, but D says \"the most prosperous\" which might not be supported. The text doesn't say that. The expansion of geography knowledge (i.e., mapping) is the focus. The key point is that the expansion (geography) reflects transportation progress leading to Zheng He's later navigation. So the answer should relate to that. The options are about the yuan dynasty's position in history. The text doesn't say that the yuan's trade was the most prosperous, so D is probably not correct.\n\nWait, maybe I missed something. Let's check the original text again. It says: \"The expansion of overseas geography knowledge reflects the progress of overseas transportation. It can be considered that the Overseas activities of the Yuan Dynasty laid the foundation for Zheng He's navigation in the 15th century.\"\n\nSo the expansion (geography) led to Zheng He's navigation. The question is about which statement is consistent. The options are about the yuan dynasty's status in history. The text doesn't mention anything about the yuan's trade being the most prosperous or the most developed. So D and C are out. B is about the western expedition, which isn't mentioned. A is about the number of countries, which isn't compared. So none of the options seem correct? But that can't be. Wait, maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, the text says \"the Yuan Dynasty inherited the policy of opening to the outside world in the Tang and Song dynasties, strengthened its political ties with overseas countries, and actively carried out overseas trade economically.\" So compared to previous dynasties (Tang, Song), the Yuan were more active in opening, strengthening ties, and carrying out trade. So maybe A is correct if \"far more\" is referring to the number of countries they had contact with compared to previous. But the text doesn't specify \"far more,\" just that they inherited and actively carried out. So maybe