be the assumption is that farmers do not want to switch, hence the need for the measure. But the options don't directly state that.\n\nC) Most expensive methods use other aeration. Not sure how this is related. The argument is about farmers switching from deep tillage to no-till. If C is saying that expensive methods are alternatives, but farmers can't switch because they are expensive, then C might be part of the assumption. But the argument is presented as a reason to switch, so the assumption would be that farmers can switch. But C is about the most expensive, which might not be relevant.\n\nD) Tilling other than deep tillage is not a viable option. If D is true, then farmers can't switch, which would make the argument's conclusion invalid. But the argument is presented as a reason to switch, so if D is an assumption, then the argument is assuming that farmers can switch. But D says that only deep tillage is viable, which would contradict the argument. Therefore, D is likely the assumption that the argument is based on. Because if D were true, then farmers can't switch, so the argument's conclusion that they should switch is wrong. But the question is about which assumption the argument depends on. The argument is presented as a reason to switch, so the assumption would be the reason why farmers don't switch. If D is saying that only deep tillage is allowed, then farmers have no choice but to use it, making the argument's conclusion invalid. But the argument is presented as a reason to switch, so the assumption must be that farmers are considering switching but have no other options. Therefore, D is the assumption. So the answer is D.\n</think>\n\nThe argument presented claims that deep tillage is even more deleterious to topsoil than no-till methods, implying farmers should switch to no-till. The key assumption under this reasoning is that farmers lack viable alternatives to deep tillage. Option **D** states that \"tilling by any method other than deep tillage is not a viable option,\" which would invalidate the argument's conclusion. If farmers have no other methods, they cannot reduce erosion risks, making the recommendation for a switch moot. Thus, the argument hinges on the implicit assumption that no valid alternative exists beyond deep tillage.\n\n**Answer:** D  \n\\boxed{D}",
