is possible only by great scientists, then maybe not. Wait, the passage doesn't specify that only great scientists can create variations. It just says that every idea is a variation. So if a great scientist's idea is a variation, then A would be true. But the problem is that the passage doesn't state that all great scientists can do this. It's possible that only some do. So maybe A is not necessarily true. Wait, but the question is which must also be true EXCEPT. So if the passage says that every idea is a variation, then any idea (including those called \"great scientists\") would be a variation. So A would be true. But maybe the answer is A is not the except one. Let's check other options.\n\nB) Careful analysis of a specific variation can reveal previous themes of which it is a variation.\nThe passage says that careful analysis leads to understanding that new ideas are variations of previous ones. So if you analyze a specific variation, you can figure out the previous theme it's a variation of. So B seems true. So B must be true, so it's not the except one.\n\nC) Some new scientific discoveries do not represent, on a deep level, a variation on previous themes.\nThe passage states that every new idea is a variation. So C is contradicting the passage. Therefore, C must be false, so C is the except one. Wait, but let me check again. The passage says \"every idea is built upon a thousand related ideas,\" which would mean every idea is a variation. So C claims that some new discoveries are not variations, which contradicts the passage. Therefore, C is false, so it's the except one. So the answer would be C.\n\nD) No scientific idea is entirely independent of all other ideas.\nThe passage says that every idea is built upon related ideas, so they are dependent. Therefore, D is true. So D must be true, so it's not the except one.\n\nSo, the options are A, B, C, D. The question asks which must also be true EXCEPT. So the answer is the one that is not necessarily true. From the above, C is false because the passage says every idea is a variation. Therefore, C is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe passage emphasizes that creativity in science hinges on generating variations of existing concepts. Here's the breakdown of the options:\n\n- **A)** Claims all \"great scientific discoverers\" can create variations. While true for any idea (as per the passage), the statement doesn't explicitly restrict this ability to \"great scientists.\" However, the question asks for the exception.  \n- **B)** States analyzing a specific variation reveals deeper connections. The passage supports this, making **B** true.  \n- **C)** asserts some new discoveries aren't variations. This contradicts the passage's core claim that **every** idea is a variation. Thus, **C** is false and the exception.  \n- **D)** Positions scientific ideas as interdependent. The passage confirms dependencies, making **D** true.  \n\nThe exception is **C**, as it directly contradicts the passage's assertion that \"every idea is built upon a thousand related ideas.\"  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{C}",
