|\u00acE|, which would mean |E| - |\u00acE| > 0. But since |E| + |\u00acE| = |P| + |B|, then |E| > |\u00acE| implies |E| > (|E| + |\u00acE|)/2. Wait, maybe not. Let's think with numbers. Suppose |P| = 10, |B| = 10. If P and B are disjoint, then total tools are 20. Suppose |B engraving| = 5, |B non-engraving| = 5. Then, |E| = 10 + 5 = 15, |\u00acE| = 5. 15 > 5. If |B engraving| = 6, |B non-engraving| = 4. Then, |E| = 16, |\u00acE| = 4. 16 > 4. If |B engraving| = 5, |B non-engraving| = 10. Then, |E| = 15, |\u00acE| = 10. 15 > 10. Wait, but if |B engraving| = 5 and |B non-engraving| = 10, then total tools are 15 (P) + 15 (B) = 30. Wait, no. If P is 10, B is 10, and they are disjoint, total is 20. If |B engraving| = 5, |B non-engraving| = 5, then |E| = 10 + 5 = 15, |\u00acE| = 5. If |B engraving| = 5, |B non-engraving| = 10, that's impossible because B is only 10 tools. So, in reality, |B non-engraving| can't exceed |B|. So, in this case, if we assume B (that P and B are disjoint), then the conclusion |E| > |\u00acE| would hold as long as |E| > |\u00acE|. But since |E| = |P| + |B engraving| and |\u00acE| = |B non-engraving|, and since |B non-engraving| \u2264 |B engraving| (because B is split into two parts), then |E| = |P| + |B engrving| \u2265 |B engrving| + |B engrving| = 2|B engrving|. But |\u00acE| = |B non-engraving| \u2264 |B engrving|. So, |E| \u2265 2|B engrving|, and |\u00acE| \u2264 |B engrving|. Therefore, |E| \u2265 2|B engrving| and |\u00acE| \u2264 |B engrving|. Therefore, |E| \u2265 2|B engrving| \u2265 |\u00acE| (since |B engrving| \u2265 |\u00acE|). Wait, no. If |B engrving| is, say, 10, then |\u00acE| is \u2264 10. But |E| is 10 + 10 = 20, which is greater than |\u00acE| = 10. If |B engrving| is 5, |\u00acE| is \u2264 5, and |E| is 10 + 5 = 15 > 5. So, in all cases where B is split, and P and B are disjoint, the conclusion |E| > |\u00acE| holds. Because |E| = |P| + |B engrving|, and |\u00acE| = |B non-engraving|. Since |B non-engraving| \u2264 |B engr