rack than land-based, but their patterns aren't as easily understood, then this supports the idea that studying the sea floor is more complex. Because even though there are currents, you can't see them as clearly as you can see land. So B could be a candidate.\n\nOption C: If the temperature is stable, then studying temperature changes would be harder. But the original context is about extensive study, not just temperature. So maybe C is not directly related. Unless the stability implies less variability, making it harder to detect changes. But the original problem is about the difficulty of extensive study, not just temperature. So maybe C is not the best.\n\nOption D: If only a few people can see the maps even though they're in libraries, that implies that having the maps isn't practical. So studying them would be difficult. This supports the idea that studying the sea floor is hard because you need access to specialized information and tools. So D seems strong.\n\nBut wait, the options are about what would best support the discussion. The discussion is that studying the sea floor is more difficult. So we need an option that adds more evidence for that.\n\nOption B talks about the difficulty of tracking ocean currents compared to land. If ocean currents are more complex and their patterns aren't as easily understood, that would mean that studying them is more difficult. So if you can't easily see the patterns, that adds to the difficulty. So B could be a good answer.\n\nOption D talks about the lack of practicality of accessing the maps. Even though they are in libraries, you need specific tools to interpret them. So studying them would be difficult. So D also supports the idea.\n\nSo now, which is better? B or D?\n\nThe original context says that scientists can't see the sea floor without instruments. So the problem is that they can't observe it directly. So the key is that studying the sea floor requires using instruments, which complicates things. So options that talk about the difficulty in using instruments would support that.\n\nOption B mentions that ocean currents are harder to track than land. But the original problem is about the difficulty of studying the sea floor in general, not specifically currents. So maybe B is more about a specific aspect.\n\nOption D talks about the lack of practicality of the maps. So even though the data exists, you need specialized tools to use them. So studying them would be difficult. So D is about the difficulty in obtaining and using information.\n\nThe original discussion is about the extensive study of the sea floor being difficult. So both B and D could support that. But which is stronger?\n\nThe question is to choose the best support. The answer is the one that, if true, would most effectively confirm the original statement.\n\nOption C: If the temperature is stable, then studying temperature changes would be harder. But the original problem is not about temperature changes, but about the difficulty of extensive study. So maybe C is not the best.\n\nOption A is about mountains below sea level, which is confusing. So probably not.\n\nSo between B and D.\n\nOption B: powerful currents circulate on the ocean floor, but the overall pattern is not as easily understood as land-based. So if you can't see the pattern, that's harder. So this supports the idea that studying the sea floor is difficult.\n\nOption D: very few people can see the maps even though they are in libraries. So the maps are available but not practical. So studying them would be difficult.\n\nWhich is better? The problem is that both B and D could be correct. But maybe the answer is D because the original context emphasizes that you can't see the sea floor without instruments. So the key is that you need to use instruments, which are hard to come by. So D talks about the lack of practicality of the maps, implying that studying them is difficult. So D would be a good answer.\n\nAlternatively, B talks about the complexity of tracking currents. But the original problem is about the difficulty of studying the sea floor in general, not specifically currents. So maybe the answer is D.\n\nWait, but the original context says \"extensive study of the sea floor be carried out. So scientists must know less about the undersea environment than any other on Earth.\" So the reason is that it's hard to see. So the answer should be an option that shows that studying the sea floor is hard because of lack of visibility or access.\n\nOption D: \"Very few people have seen detailed maps of sea floor extensions, even though such maps can be seen in almost every major library.\" So even though the maps exist, they are not practical to use. So studying them would require specialized tools. So this supports the idea that studying the sea floor is difficult.\n\nOption B: \"Powerful currents circulate on the ocean floor, but the overall pattern of their movement is not as easily understood as that of air currents on land.\" This is talking about the difficulty of tracking currents, which is part of studying the sea floor. But the original problem is about the difficulty of extensive study. So maybe B is also a valid answer.\n\nBut the question is to choose the best support. The answer is the one that, when added, most effectively confirms the original statement.\n\nThe original statement is that studying the sea floor is more difficult. The options are about different aspects. Let's see:\n\nA) Irrelevant, since mountains below sea level aren't mountains.\n\nB) Talks about difficulty in tracking currents, which is part of studying the sea floor, but the original problem is about the difficulty of extensive study. So maybe B is a possible answer but not the best.\n\nC) Talks about stability of temperature, which might not be directly related.\n\nD) Talks about the lack of practicality of maps, which is about the difficulty in obtaining and using information. This directly supports the idea that studying the